[section:intro Introduction]

Boost.Taar is a portable, header-only C++23 library that provides building
blocks for writing HTTP servers and clients on top of __Asio__, __Beast__,
__Url__, and __Json__.

The library is organised around four ideas:

* A [link taar.intro.matchers /matcher DSL/] for selecting which handler
  should serve a given HTTP request.
* A [link taar.intro.handlers /handler/] family that adapts ordinary
  callables into HTTP message generators, with automatic argument
  extraction from path templates, query parameters, headers, cookies, and
  message bodies.
* An [link taar.intro.session HTTP session coroutine] that owns the
  request/response lifecycle for one connection, dispatching to registered
  matcher + handler pairs and reporting soft and hard errors through
  pluggable handlers.
* A [link taar.intro.server TCP server coroutine] that accepts connections
  and spawns an HTTP session per connection, integrated with Asio's
  cancellation machinery.

The library is header-only and does not require linking against a Taar
binary. It does depend on the compiled Boost libraries that __Asio__,
__Beast__, __Url__, __Json__, and __System__ require.

[heading:matchers Matchers]

A matcher is a small object that inspects an HTTP request and answers the
question /does this request belong to me?/. Matchers compose with the usual
boolean operators (`&&`, `||`, `!`) and with relational operators
(`==`, `!=`) where it makes sense:

    method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/sum/{a}/{b}"

[heading:handlers Handlers]

A handler turns an HTTP request into an HTTP response. The library ships
with two adapters:

* [link taar.ref.boost__taar__handler__rest `rest`] wraps a plain callable.
  Arguments declared on the callable are produced by
  [link taar.ref.boost__taar__handler /argument providers/] such as
  `path_arg`, `query_arg`, `header_arg`, `cookie_arg`, and `body_arg`. The
  return value of the callable is converted into an HTTP response via the
  `tag_invoke`-based [link taar.ref.boost__taar__core__response_from
  `response_from`] extension point, so user-defined return types are
  supported out of the box.
* [link taar.ref.boost__taar__handler__htdocs `htdocs`] serves static
  documents from a root directory, with content-type inferred from the
  file extension.

You can also supply a hand-written callable with the signature
`(request, context) -> http::message_generator`.

[heading:session Sessions]

The [link taar.ref.boost__taar__session__http `session::http`] coroutine
holds a list of /(matcher, handler)/ pairs registered through
`register_request_handler`. For each incoming request the session walks the
list, runs the first matching handler, writes the response, and continues
to the next request on the same keep-alive connection.

The session distinguishes /soft errors/ (recoverable, application-visible
problems like a missing query parameter that can be turned into a 400
response) from /hard errors/ (connection-level problems that take the
connection down). Both have pluggable handlers, defaulting to sane
behaviour.

[heading:server Servers]

The [link taar.ref.boost__taar__server__tcp `server::tcp`] coroutine binds,
listens, and accepts connections, spawning one
[link taar.intro.session HTTP session] per connection. It integrates with
Asio cancellation signals so a single signal can shut down the whole
server.

[endsect]
